# molt serving config — Tier-A sandbox surface (agent-editable). SPEC §6 seed.
# One arg (or arg value-pair) per line; comments/blank lines stripped by the runner.
# The runner supplies: -m <gguf> --host 127.0.0.1 --port <port> --alias molt.
-ngl 99
# ALL experts CPU-side for first light. Phase-0 run 1 taught the layer-split gotcha:
# --n-cpu-moe N puts the GPU-expert layers at the TAIL (N..59), and the default even layer
# split hands the whole tail to GPU1 -> 25.7 GiB on one 24 GB card -> OOM (see
# notes/logs/serve-phase0-1.log). Walking experts onto the GPUs NEEDS a matching
# --tensor-split (boundary inside the expert region), e.g.:
#   --n-cpu-moe 54 -ts 54,6   (~6 expert layers on GPU1 tail, GPU0 takes 54 lean layers)
# That's Tier-A experiment #1. Non-expert-only load: ~8 GB/card incl. KV + scratch.
--cpu-moe
# batch geometry: prefill cost on this box = (streams of CPU-resident expert weights) —
# bigger ubatch = fewer streams per token (P2 lesson; Phase-0 run 1 measured 129 t/s prefill
# at ub 2048 cold — ub 4096 halves per-token streaming cost). Tier-A tunable.
-b 8192
-ub 8192
-fa on
--cache-type-k q8_0
--cache-type-v q8_0
# 4 slots x 40960 ctx: parallel eval (referee runs 4 workers); ctx32k probe (38,273 tok) fits
-c 163840
-np 4
# SMT threads for batch/prefill matmuls (decode threads stay at physical-core default)
-tb 32
--jinja
--chat-template-file models/hf-bf16/chat_template.jinja
--reasoning-format auto
# BOUNDED thinking (decision 2026-07-02, data: smoke_06 thought 9.3K chars into a 3072 cap
# without answering; unbounded think at 12 t/s makes evals unbounded). 1024 think tokens is
# generous for tool calls; part of the optimized serving config — hold constant across
# compared configs, tune only as an explicit Tier-A experiment.
--reasoning-budget 1024
# MTP speculative decode is UNAVAILABLE for this model: Ornith's config declares an MTP head
# but neither the BF16 nor the FP8 repo ships its weights (verified 2026-07-02, TODO decision
# log). SPEC §3's draft-mtp A/B is off the table unless DeepReinforce publishes them.
# Model-free speculative alternatives worth a Tier-A experiment instead:
# --spec-type ngram-simple      # (or ngram-map-k / ngram-mod / ngram-cache)
